      "id": 1,
      "text": "La Puente means \"the bridge\" in old Spanish, named after a bridge Portola’s 1769 expedition built over San Jose Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://lapuente.org/city-facts/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city grew around Rancho La Puente, a 48,000‑acre estate granted in 1842 to John Rowland and William Workman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Puente%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1930s La Puente housed the world’s largest walnut packing plant, reflecting its historic fruit‑grove economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Puente%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Padma Lakshmi, the Indian‑American author and television host, was born in La Puente, adding a touch of global celebrity to the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Puente%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Workman and Temple Family Homestead Museum in nearby Industry preserves early La Puente history, showing the area’s ranching roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
